// tests/samples/ls_opt_env_test.lls — discriminator for "did the JIT actually
// run the optimization pipeline?", used by tests/ls_opt_env.cmake to verify
// that LS_OPT reaches the JIT (`lls run`) and that the CLI `-O<n>` flag wins
// over it.
//
// There is no diagnostic surface that reports the JIT's effective -O level, so
// this sample instead relies on a *behavioural* difference: the optimizer is
// allowed to reassociate/contract this floating-point accumulation, which
// changes the low bits of the result. The residual against the exact decimal
// answer is amplified by 1e18 so that @print's six-digit precision can show
// it -- the raw sum prints as a flat 0.800000 at every level.
//
// The test driver never hardcodes the two magic numbers (they depend on the
// host CPU and LLVM version); it only asserts that O0 and O2 disagree and that
// the LS_OPT / -O runs land on the right side of that divide. If a future LLVM
// stops producing any difference here, the driver fails loudly and asks for a
// new observable rather than silently degrading into a test that proves
// nothing.
//
// Deliberately float-only and tiny: no heap, no stdlib imports, so the sample
// stays fast (it is run four times per test) and cannot be perturbed by
// ownership/container switches.

def fma_chain(f64 a, f64 b, f64 c) -> f64 {
    return a * b + c
}

def main() {
    f64 acc = 0.0
    for i in 0..40 {
        acc = fma_chain(0.1, 0.2, acc)
    }
    @print((acc - 0.8) * 1000000000000000000.0)
}
